<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Bharatpur State</h1><p class="st-meta">1722 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Bharatpur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Jat kingdom of eastern Rajasthan whose mud-walled fortress of Lohagarh famously withstood a British siege in 1805.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jats">Jat</a> peasant leaders around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> rose against Mughal authority in the late 17th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badan-singh">Badan Singh</a>, recognised as chief around 1722, consolidated the clans and fortified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deeg">Deeg</a> and Bharatpur. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suraj-mal">Suraj Mal</a> (ruled 1755–63) turned the chiefship into a major power, building the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lohagarh-fort">Lohagarh</a> fort and capturing Agra in 1761.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Bharatpur was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jats">Jat</a> monarchy with a strong military tradition and a wealthy farming base. Its forts at Bharatpur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deeg">Deeg</a>, with the Deeg water palaces, reflect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suraj-mal">Suraj Mal's</a> ambitions. In 1805 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerard-lake-1st-viscount-lake">Lord Lake</a> failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lohagarh-fort">Lohagarh</a> after four assaults; the fort finally fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stapleton-cotton-1st-viscount-combermere">Lord Combermere</a> in 1826, when the British intervened in a succession dispute and installed the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balwant-singh-of-bharatpur">Balwant Singh</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keoladeo-national-park">Keoladeo</a> wetlands were kept as a royal duck-shooting reserve.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After independence Bharatpur joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/matsya-union">Matsya Union</a> in March 1948, which merged into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan</a> in 1949. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keoladeo-national-park">Keoladeo</a> is now a national park and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bharatpur-state.jpg" alt="Bharatpur State" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday (talk) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bharatpur.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1722 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bharatpur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Badan Singh, Suraj Mal, Jawahar Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Braj Bhasha, Hindi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bharatpur</dd></dl></aside></div>
